{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790361397,"nonce":"9bZ0oRCIhwbD8gOfqtky/064skA=","title":"Cancelling an async task leaks its open handler state and runs none of its cleanup","message":"When a driver cancels a task (`drive_until` for a promise marked cancelled, `PromiseRace::await_first` for the losers, `await_with_timeout` for the overrunner, all in crates/zyntax_embed/src/runtime/promise.rs), `host_futures::deregister_task` drops the task's parked futures and timers, frees the fibers it created (`krio_adapter::fiber::free_task_fibers`) and forgets its handler-stack segment (`effect_runtime::task_handler_forget`). Forgetting the segment discards the frames without freeing the `H$state` regions their `with` scopes allocated, and none of the task's own exit paths run, so what the task holds is released in whatever order the registries hold it rather than in reverse acquisition order. `ZyntaxPromise::cancel` on its own only marks the promise; the teardown happens only when a driver sees the mark.\n\nTask identity is also loose (found by reading): a task id is the promise's index in the slice the driver was given, `drive_until` leaves `CURRENT_TASK_ID` (and the fiber backend's `FIBER_TASK_ID`) at the last id it stamped, and 0 is both the first task and the \"no task\" value fibers made outside any task are filed under. `await_with_timeout` always deregisters task 0. A fiber made outside async code after a drive can therefore be attributed to a task id that a later cancellation frees.\n\nDone: cancellation releases what the task holds in reverse acquisition order (fibers before the handler frames that could resume them, then each open scope's state), task ids are unique for the life of the runtime with a distinct \"no task\" value, and a test cancels a task inside a stateful `with` that owns a fiber and checks segments, fibers and state regions are all gone.","files":null}]}